# The post-merge review pass

This file's instructions are duplicated, pithily, into `coordinator-core.md` for a harness that cannot load this file directly.
TheForge's coordinator-core ADR records why that file exists and what must survive every edit to it.
A change here updates `.forge/coordinator-core.md` in the same change.
TheForge's `lock` check fails until the core section citing this one is re-read and the lock refreshed.

The post-merge review is this project's second review and its only independent one: higher-tier reviewers, never the builder, re-review every PR merged to `main` since a watermark.
They look for what a per-PR review structurally cannot see: cross-PR interactions, migrations, resource exhaustion that only shows under real execution, and guard tests that cannot fail.

The procedure is `.agents/skills/post-merge-review/SKILL.md`; this file states the rules around it.

## The tracker

The post-merge review tracker {{ pmr_ref }} is a standing issue holding the reviews still to be done and those done (SDLC-095).
When a PR merges, the agent that merged it, or for an owner's merge the agent that opened it, posts a pending note on the tracker as a comment, and a run's archive comment lists the notes it covered (`.agents/skills/post-merge-review/SKILL.md`, "Pending reviews").
Its body holds only the watermark, a pointer to the procedure, and the run log index.
Each run's detail goes in its own archive comment, never the body.
The tracker never closes, and no PR or commit message may put a closing keyword next to its number (`.forge/tickets-and-branches.md`, "Closing keywords").

## Cadence

A pass runs after roughly every 10 PRs merged to `main`, and at least every 2 to 3 days of active development, whichever comes first.
The Main Health thread runs it on its own scheduled routine (`.agents/skills/watch-main-ci/SKILL.md`), skipping a firing when nothing has merged since the watermark.
A feature's post-merge review pass ticket, filed at pipeline stage 6, also triggers a pass once the feature's terminal slices have merged; the standing cadence is a safety net under those tickets, not a substitute.

## Who runs it

The Main Health thread orchestrates the pass at build-tier.
Review-tier sub-agents do the reviewing, one per subsystem batch plus one for the window's merge-written text, each in its own private worktree.
Each sub-agent reports the model it actually ran on, and the run's archive comment records every batch's performer and tier.
The pass is file-only: it opens issues, never fix PRs.

## The security axis

Every pass also reviews every surviving line through STRIDE (spoofing, tampering, repudiation, information disclosure, denial of service, elevation of privilege) against `docs/security/threat-model.md` and the other security docs, asking whether it added attack surface, weakened a control a security doc names, made a security-doc statement untrue, or skipped its design's security part.
The reviewer finds gaps and does not fill them.
When the code is the problem, the finding is graded like any other; when only a security doc is out of date, it is a Should whose ticket points at `security-docs`' reconcile mode.
Each reviewer's brief names the security axis explicitly, because a brief that leaves it implied gets a review that skips security.

Some text reaches `main` without being a line in a file's diff: a PR's title and body, and the commit messages a squash or merge writes.
The security axis covers this merge-written text for every PR in the window, asking whether any of it disclosed something it shouldn't, or put a closing keyword next to the wrong issue or PR.
Such a finding is graded like any other; fixing text already on `main` (editing a PR body, a history rewrite) is the project's own call, outside this file-only pass.

## Governed artefacts

For every file in its batch that the artefact map (`.forge/agent-skills-and-reference.md`) lists, each reviewer's brief names the governing skill, and the reviewer checks the surviving lines against it and against the file's own Conventions section where it has one (SDLC-119).
A brief that leaves it implied gets a review that skips it.

## Findings

Findings are classified Must, Should, Could or Won't (`.agents/skills/code-review/SKILL.md`, "Triaging findings (MoSCoW)").
Must and Should findings are fixed; Could and Won't are one-liners in the run's archive comment, with no ticket.
Each Must gets its own ticket, so it never waits inside a bundle behind lower-severity work.
Should findings are grouped by file or feature area into one ticket per theme.
A finding that is a genuine open design question, with no fix sketch, goes to the owner as its own `needs-decision` ticket, and the owner decides whether it is addressed or parked as a `deferred-arch-finding` under the architecture-findings epic {{ arch_ref }} (SDLC-039).
When two or more filed findings share a shape, the run asks the owner "is there a principle here?".

## A repeat offender becomes a self-review pattern

When a run finds a kind of defect earlier runs already found, the same follow-up that files the findings adds the pattern to the self-review checklist (`.agents/skills/self-review/SKILL.md`), so the builder's own review catches it next time.

## Watermark discipline

A run that did not cover its whole window does not advance the watermark, and still appends a run log row, marked partial, so the gap stays visible.

## Mutation sampling

Every fifth pass may also sample a few modules with a mutation-testing tool to find tests that kill no mutants: an audit, never a gate, run only with the owner's explicit go-ahead on that pass.
